WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.800
SpaceX turns 20 years old in a few months, and&nbsp;
Elon has certainly made a lot of progress in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.600
that time, but big things have small beginnings.&nbsp;
The early days were incredibly difficult for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:15.280
company: test launches kept failing, suppliers&nbsp;
weren’t delivering, and bankruptcy was always just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:20.000
around the corner. There was even a group of drunk&nbsp;
Russians who almost ruined everything, but we’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.640
get to that story in a minute. The early history&nbsp;
of SpaceX is a powerful lesson in the importance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.840
of perseverance, so let’s go back to the year&nbsp;
two thousand and the very beginning of SpaceX.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.200
It was October, and Elon Musk, who was&nbsp;
serving as the CEO of PayPal at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.600
was in Sydney, Australia to watch the Olympic&nbsp;
Games with his girlfriend. This wasn’t great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:47.280
timing though. PayPal was in turmoil as executives&nbsp;
and investors argued over which direction to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.320
the company. This battle came to a head while he&nbsp;
was still 7 thousand miles away from the office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.520
and by the time he made it back to Palo Alto, he&nbsp;
was out of a job. Getting fired from PayPal was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:03.040
definitely not part of Elon’s plan, but there was&nbsp;
a silver lining. He had always been interested in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.680
space travel and now that he wasn’t in charge of&nbsp;
a growing company, he could spend as much time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.480
as he wanted exploring his true passions. It was a&nbsp;
bit of a blessing in disguise, since what he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:17.360
go on to build at SpaceX would be dramatically&nbsp;
more ambitious than PayPal, but it would require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:22.240
a ton of work, so he started studying. First,&nbsp;
he joined a space-advocacy non-profit called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.800
the Mars Society. The organization was filled&nbsp;
with tons of scientists and researchers who were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:32.240
obsessed with space travel and the colonization&nbsp;
of Mars in particular. Elon didn’t want to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.960
talk about space exploration though, he actually&nbsp;
wanted to do it. So he started pitching an idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.800
he called the “Mars Oasis.” His plan was to send&nbsp;
a small greenhouse to Mars that would grow plants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.760
in Martian soil. Elon believed that if people&nbsp;
saw concrete evidence that life was possible on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.800
the red planet, it would reinvigorate interest&nbsp;
in space exploration. After we landed a man on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.240
the moon, and the space race came to a close,&nbsp;
NASA funding fell significantly, and no one was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:03.600
seriously working on Mars colonization. So Elon&nbsp;
got to work. He needed a rocket, and not just any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:09.680
rocket, a big one. So he assembled a crack team of&nbsp;
experts to help him find the ideal rocket. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:14.720
he called Adeo Ressi, who was one of his friends&nbsp;
from UPenn back in his college days, but Adeo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:20.240
didn’t have any space experience, so Elon called&nbsp;
Jim Cantrell. Cantrell had worked on various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.200
joint missile defense programs conducted between&nbsp;
America and Russia and was perfect for this job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:30.080
there was only one problem: he thought Elon was&nbsp;
crazy. See, when Cantrell first picked up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.120
phone, Elon told him that he was a billionaire,&nbsp;
which he wasn’t yet, who wanted to save humanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.800
by sending rockets to Mars. It sounded like the&nbsp;
ravings of a madman, so Cantrell was very wary to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:46.160
meet Elon in person. Just to be safe, he insisted&nbsp;
that they meet at the Denver Airport behind the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:50.640
security checkpoint, so he could guarantee&nbsp;
that Elon wouldn’t have any weapons on him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:55.040
Fortunately, once they sat down and had a&nbsp;
chance to talk through Elon’s full vision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:59.600
Cantrell was convinced and agreed to help&nbsp;
out. So the three of them flew to Paris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:03.600
where they met with a French aerospace&nbsp;
company to discuss the purchase of a rocket.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.720
But the only rockets available cost over a hundred&nbsp;
million dollars each. Even though Elon was rich,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:14.160
he wasn’t that rich yet. He only had about $30&nbsp;
million dollars which he had made from the sale of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:20.480
his first company Zip2, so he had to find another&nbsp;
option. The team then flew from Paris to Moscow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:25.360
in hopes of finding a cheaper rocket. After&nbsp;
the fall of the Soviet Union, demilitarization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.160
treaties required that Russia decommission&nbsp;
thousands of intercontinental ballistic missiles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:35.600
or ICBMs. These were well designed rockets, and&nbsp;
after you removed the nuclear warhead from them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.440
they could definitely be used as orbital&nbsp;
launch vehicles. While they were in Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:43.680
Elon and his team scheduled meetings with&nbsp;
two separate Russian space organizations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:47.920
They thought it would be easy to buy an old ICBM,&nbsp;
since they were basically just sitting around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:52.800
useless at this point, but negotiation with the&nbsp;
Russians turned out to be close to impossible. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.520
really enjoyed Adeo’s account of the whole fiasco,&nbsp;
he said: “Then we started having meetings with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.080
the Russian space program, which is basically&nbsp;
fueled by vodka. We'd all go into this little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:06.880
room and every single person had his own bottle&nbsp;
in front of him. They'd toast every two minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:12.720
which means twenty or thirty toasts an hour.&nbsp;
'To space!' 'To America!' 'To America in space!'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.600
I finally looked over at Elon and Jim and they&nbsp;
were passed out on the table. Then I passed out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:22.160
myself.” It seemed like a dead-end, but Elon&nbsp;
was willing to do anything to get a deal done.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.640
He spent hours talking to the Russians just in&nbsp;
order to get them to open up a bit about what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.280
actually had in stock. And they weren’t exactly&nbsp;
friendly to his inquiries. During one particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:37.120
heated exchange, one of the Russians actually spit&nbsp;
on Elon in disgust. But even all those ridiculous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.160
events in Russia didn’t end negotiations entirely.&nbsp;
For the next meeting, the Russians flew to Los&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:47.120
Angeles to meet with Elon on his home turf, but&nbsp;
they seemed more interested in partying than doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:52.640
business. They reportedly called him and asked for&nbsp;
$5,000 in cash to go clubbing for the night. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:58.000
it’s not exactly easy to withdraw 5 grand from&nbsp;
a bank on a weekend, and ATMs have limits. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:03.200
Elon’s friend Adeo knew someone who worked at the&nbsp;
Mondrian in West Hollywood and kept lots of cash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:07.520
on-hand. It must have been a ridiculous favor&nbsp;
to ask someone to borrow thousands of dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.400
from the hotel safe, but it worked. The Russians&nbsp;
were appeased and spent the night partying. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:18.160
their third meeting, Elon was more determined than&nbsp;
ever. It was February of 2002 and at this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:22.880
Elon had been trying to find a rocket for over&nbsp;
a year with no luck. So he flew to Russia, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:28.160
this time, he brought Mike Griffin. This guy was&nbsp;
a space industry veteran and actually went on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:33.840
lead all of NASA in 2005. Griffin brought a level&nbsp;
of seriousness to the discussions that helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:39.120
move negotiations forward and Elon was finally&nbsp;
able to get a quote. There was only one problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.760
it was still too high. The Russians wanted $8&nbsp;
million dollars for a single rocket. Elon was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:48.720
willing to pay the $8 million dollars, but for&nbsp;
that price, he wanted two rockets. The Russians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:53.120
held firm to their original pricing and wouldn’t&nbsp;
cut a deal with Elon, so he stormed out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:58.000
meeting into the freezing Moscow air outside and&nbsp;
took a cab back to the airport. On the flight back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.520
to America, Elon’s team ordered a round of drinks&nbsp;
to celebrate getting out of Russia alive. But Elon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:08.320
wasn’t celebrating, he had his laptop open and was&nbsp;
already thinking about his next step. He started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:13.040
building an Excel model for rocket construction&nbsp;
costs, and he found something incredible. Only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:17.520
3 percent of the cost of a rocket came from&nbsp;
raw materials like aluminium and copper. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:22.400
were a ton of inefficiencies, most of which could&nbsp;
be eliminated with careful planning. Historically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.200
the space industry has operated using a cost-plus&nbsp;
model, which means that the rocket manufacturer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:32.800
will total up their costs and then add a profit&nbsp;
margin on top. This leads to perverse incentives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:37.120
though, since the higher your costs are, the&nbsp;
higher your profits will be. And governments are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:42.560
notoriously bad at demanding lower costs. On that&nbsp;
flight, Elon discovered that if he streamlined the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:47.520
entire rocket construction process and cut out&nbsp;
the inefficient subcontractors, he could get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:52.160
to orbit for a fraction of the industry-standard&nbsp;
cost. Even though Elon started by planning to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:57.360
straight to Mars, realistically, he had to start&nbsp;
smaller. So he settled on a small-payload rocket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:01.760
where he could prove out his ideas before going&nbsp;
bigger. He’d been tracking the industry for years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:07.680
and found that, until the 1990s, the vast majority&nbsp;
of launches were transporting large satellites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:12.640
usually for government agencies. Elon couldn’t&nbsp;
afford to build a rocket big enough to compete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:18.160
for these types of contracts, at least initially,&nbsp;
but satellites were getting smaller. Increasingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:22.240
smaller telecommunications satellites were&nbsp;
providing critical connectivity to private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:27.680
sector companies and Elon believed that for around&nbsp;
$100 million, he could build a rocket that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:32.560
get those satellites to orbit. That still wasn’t&nbsp;
a ton of money, and a good portion of that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:37.760
going to need to go toward designing the rocket&nbsp;
engine. Luckily, Elon found the perfect partner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:42.320
His name was Tom Mueller, and he’d been obsessed&nbsp;
with building model rockets since his childhood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:46.240
After graduating from the University of Idaho&nbsp;
with a degree in mechanical engineering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:51.760
Mueller moved to California and was hired by TRW.&nbsp;
While there, Mueller developed several advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:59.360
liquid rocket engines, most notably the TR-106,&nbsp;
which generated 650 thousand pounds of thrust.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:03.600
It was a huge achievement, but after&nbsp;
15 years climbing the corporate ladder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:08.240
he was ready for a change. Mueller wanted&nbsp;
to move faster and try out new ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:14.640
but TRW employed over 100 thousand people and was&nbsp;
hesitant to take big risks. So Mueller joined a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:19.760
group of amature rocket enthusiasts called the&nbsp;
Reaction Research Society. Everyone had a real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:24.640
hacker mindset and would drive out to the Mojave&nbsp;
Desert to launch rockets they’d build in their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:29.840
garages. While Mueller was at one of these events,&nbsp;
he met a man named John Garvey. The two hit it off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:34.880
immediately and started working together on nights&nbsp;
and weekends on a completely new idea. They called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:40.400
the BFR, which, if you follow SpaceX at all, you&nbsp;
probably know what that stands for already. Now we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:46.160
don’t know for sure, but rumor has it that the BFR&nbsp;
was the largest amature rocket engine ever built.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:51.280
It could generate 12 thousand pounds of thrust,&nbsp;
and it was enough to impress Elon when he saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:56.800
it in person in early 2002. It wasn’t quite strong&nbsp;
enough, but it was close. Elon wanted to deliver a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:02.000
half-ton payload to orbit, and needed 80 thousand&nbsp;
pounds of thrust to do it, but he could tell that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:07.440
Mueller could deliver if he just had a little&nbsp;
more budget. So Elon made both Mueller and Garvey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:13.680
job offers to join SpaceX. Garvey declined, but&nbsp;
Mueller signed on as the first official employee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:18.960
and VP of Propulsion. The team wasn’t complete yet&nbsp;
though, Elon would definitely need more talented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:23.360
engineers to make his dream a reality, so he&nbsp;
started recruiting an aerospace engineer named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:27.520
Chris Thompson to work on the body and structure&nbsp;
of the rocket. Thompson had worked at Boeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:32.880
and was the perfect person for the job, so he&nbsp;
joined SpaceX as employee number 2. This new team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:37.600
immediately started planning what would become the&nbsp;
Falcon 1. The design criteria were pretty simple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:43.040
The rocket needed to deliver a half-ton satellite&nbsp;
to orbit and not much else. All the reusability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:47.760
stuff you hear about now-a-days was still a long&nbsp;
way away. Just getting off the launchpad in one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:52.560
piece would be a big enough challenge, and the&nbsp;
Falcon 1 had to do it on a shoestring budget. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:57.840
was a blessing and a curse though. Obviously it’s&nbsp;
tough to build a big rocket without a big budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:02.560
even basic materials are extremely expensive&nbsp;
after all. But the SpaceX team was working from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:07.200
a blank slate, so they didn’t have any legacy&nbsp;
technology weighing them down and they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:11.360
design completely from first principles.&nbsp;
They still needed a bit more money though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:17.040
Elon’s $30 million wasn’t quite enough. Luckily,&nbsp;
PayPal was just about to go public and Elon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:23.360
had held on to a huge stake in the company. In&nbsp;
February of 2002, the IPO went off flawlessly, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:28.960
Elon suddenly had an extra $165 million dollars&nbsp;
to play with, and he wasn’t about to just sit on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:34.320
all that cash. He immediately went out and rented&nbsp;
a 30 thousand square foot warehouse in El Segundo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:39.760
California to use as a factory. Hiring was also&nbsp;
top of mind for Elon, so he hired Hans Koenigsmann&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:45.440
as his VP of Avionics. Before joining SpaceX,&nbsp;
Koenigsmann had worked at Microcosm developing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:50.400
low-cost rockets and knew what it took to deliver&nbsp;
quickly without sacrificing on quality. The pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:56.080
were finally coming together, so Elon registered&nbsp;
SpaceX as a real company on May 6th of 2002.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:00.400
To celebrate the occasion, Elon threw a party&nbsp;
with a Mariachi band in what looks like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:04.560
completely unfurnished office. I guess people&nbsp;
don’t need office chairs if they’re dancing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:08.960
With most of the leadership team in place, Elon&nbsp;
began hiring more engineers to help with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:13.440
more technical aspects of rocket development.&nbsp;
And his strategy here was really brilliant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:18.400
he already had some solid industry veterans in&nbsp;
place, so he focused on engineering students with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:23.840
zero work experience. And he took a very hands on&nbsp;
approach here, personally calling universities to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:28.800
get in contact with the top performing students.&nbsp;
It’s hilarious to imagine getting a personal phone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:33.280
call from Elon Musk while you’re sitting in&nbsp;
your dorm room nowadays, but back then, he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:37.760
a complete no-name in the aerospace industry.&nbsp;
Taking chances on young engineering students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:43.520
allowed Elon to keep costs low and he extended&nbsp;
this thinking to his entire business. He knew that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:48.160
the space industry had been plagued by complex&nbsp;
networks of contractors and subcontractors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:53.040
If SpaceX was going to succeed, they would need to&nbsp;
do things differently. And Elon really didn’t hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:58.560
back when it came to doing things differently.&nbsp;
Nearly all rockets are manufactured vertically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:03.840
But SpaceX actually found that it would be cheaper&nbsp;
to build the entire rocket horizontally instead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:08.400
Now, there was some precedent for this, the&nbsp;
Russians did it this way, but it definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:12.880
challenged common knowledge here in the States.&nbsp;
But that’s the type of creative thinking you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:18.080
to employ if you’re going to build a rocket with&nbsp;
200 employees instead of 20 thousand. When you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:22.800
understaffed, the natural reaction is to try&nbsp;
and outsource more of the work to third-party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:27.680
contractors, but Elon really tried to do the&nbsp;
opposite. He was constantly trying to bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:33.200
any expensive or time-consuming activity in house&nbsp;
as soon as possible. SpaceX even spent $10 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:37.920
dollars building a welding workshop, just so they&nbsp;
could make more critical rocket parts in-house.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:43.360
This strategy served them well, and by early 2003,&nbsp;
the first Merlin engine was ready for testing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:47.200
but they couldn’t just turn it on in the El&nbsp;
Segundo warehouse. There’s always a risk of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:53.040
an engine exploding, so you need a big open space&nbsp;
to run a safe test. And you know what they say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:58.960
everything’s bigger in Texas. So SpaceX headed&nbsp;
to McGregor, Texas and set up shop in a defunct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:03.840
rocket testing facility. The site was pretty well&nbsp;
equipped and even had a massive concrete tripod&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:09.120
for mounting rocket engines. The only problem was&nbsp;
that McGregor was 1,400 miles from Los Angeles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:14.480
so every time the SpaceX engineers wanted to run a&nbsp;
test, they had to rent a Hummer and tow the engine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:19.440
for days to get there. It wasn’t easy, but it&nbsp;
got the job done. At least most of the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:24.560
One test was cut short by the United States&nbsp;
Secret Service. They pulled up just as the SpaceX&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:29.360
team was about to turn on the engine and stopped&nbsp;
the test. It turned out that the Merlin engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:34.080
which had been mounted on the tripod, was&nbsp;
pointing directly at President Bush’s ranch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:37.920
which wasn’t far from McGregor. They must have&nbsp;
had some pretty good intel to pick up the engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:43.680
but the incident led the SpaceX team to make&nbsp;
sure to inform the local community before each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:49.360
test fire. Even though SpaceX now had a viable&nbsp;
engine testing facility, they still needed a place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:54.160
to actually launch the rockets. They initially&nbsp;
looked at Vandenberg Air Force Base in California,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:58.880
which would have been great because it’s so close&nbsp;
to the main SpaceX headquarters. But like always,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:04.560
there was a problem. A Titan 4 rocket with a $1&nbsp;
billion dollar spy satellite on-board was sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:08.960
on the launch pad just waiting to get launched.&nbsp;
The schedule for this satellite was constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.960 --> 00:14:14.640
shifting and until it took off, SpaceX couldn’t do&nbsp;
anything. The Air Force was worried that if SpaceX&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:20.080
failed, debris from the explosion could damage the&nbsp;
billion-dollar satellite. Elon and his team spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:24.560
six months waiting for the Air Force to clear the&nbsp;
launch site, but eventually they got frustrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:28.880
and started looking for a new option. They had&nbsp;
already scouted a few potential launchpads out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:33.760
in the Pacific, and one location stood out in&nbsp;
particular. It was the Kwajalein Atoll in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:38.720
Marshall Islands and it satisfied a lot of key&nbsp;
criteria. It had already been used as a Missile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:43.920
Defense Test Site and was close to the equator.&nbsp;
So SpaceX started refurbishing the facilities in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:50.000
late 2005 and began to prepare for the launch of&nbsp;
Falcon 1. At this point SpaceX was already a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:55.760
years behind schedule. The original plan was to&nbsp;
launch the first rocket by October of 2003, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.760 --> 00:15:00.800
that didn’t matter to Elon, he was still moving&nbsp;
incredibly fast by industry standards. In early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:06.800
2006, the SpaceX team packed up the Falcon 1 and&nbsp;
shipped it to Kwajalein by boat. Once it arrived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:11.326
the engineers completed the final assembly and&nbsp;
were ready to launch. Elon gathered with his team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.326 --> 00:15:14.400
on the nearby island of Kwajalein to watch the&nbsp;
launch, and at first, everything went smoothly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:25.680
The first stage Merlin engine ignited and the&nbsp;
rocket lifted off, but just 33 seconds later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:31.040
the engine caught on fire and exploded. Even&nbsp;
though this was the first flight ever, Elon was so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:35.360
confident that it would work he had loaded&nbsp;
the rocket with a fully-functioning satellite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:39.360
which crashed through the roof of a storage shed&nbsp;
on the island. After digging through the wreckage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:43.920
engineers found the culprit responsible for&nbsp;
the explosion. It was a single corroded bolt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:48.800
which had been overlooked during inspections.&nbsp;
Even though this launch was a failure, the SpaceX&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:53.040
team still went out for drinks to celebrate the&nbsp;
milestone. They got back to work pretty quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:57.440
and just a few weeks later, the team was ready&nbsp;
for launch number two. But traveling to Kwaj&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:03.520
is not easy. The trip from Los Angeles required a&nbsp;
five hour flight to Hawaii, an overnight layover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:08.640
another flight to the Marshall Islands, and then&nbsp;
a military transport ferry to get to the actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:13.520
atoll. And island life on this tiny military&nbsp;
outpost doesn’t quite compare to Los Angeles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:18.160
Since it’s a military site, there aren’t any&nbsp;
tourists and the island basically only has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:23.040
two hotels. The rooms are run down and the&nbsp;
TV only shows military channels. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:27.520
two bars on the island and I’m sure that after&nbsp;
a few weeks the SpaceX employees basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:32.400
knew everyone on a first-name basis. But what the&nbsp;
island lacks in entertainment, it more than makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:37.120
up for with its beautiful nature. SpaceX employees&nbsp;
would spend their free time going scuba diving and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:42.080
exploring the coral reefs. During World War 2,&nbsp;
a German cruiser ship had sunk in the lagoon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:46.720
and became a beautiful artificial reef,&nbsp;
perfect for curious SpaceX divers to explore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:52.960
On March 21st, 2007, SpaceX launched their second&nbsp;
Falcon 1 rocket. This time, they were a bit more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.960 --> 00:16:58.240
realistic about their odds of success and used a&nbsp;
dummy payload instead of a customer satellite. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:03.440
first and second stages both fired successfully&nbsp;
this time, but about 4 minutes into the flight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.440 --> 00:17:08.400
the rocket started to wobble. The Merlin engine&nbsp;
kept working for another 3 minutes, but ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:13.040
failed to push the rocket into a proper orbit.&nbsp;
One year later, SpaceX tried for a third time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:17.200
and again they failed to deliver the rocket to&nbsp;
orbit. The first stage was working perfectly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:22.560
but after it separated from the second stage,&nbsp;
it kept accelerating, which caused it to smash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:25.280
into the back of the second stage. This must&nbsp;
have been a crushing defeat for the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:29.600
SpaceX team. They had been working for years on&nbsp;
this project and just couldn’t get it to work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:34.720
Even worse, the company was now about to run&nbsp;
out of money. Elon knew he was close though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:39.600
and really wanted to get one more launch together,&nbsp;
so he called up his PayPal co-founder Peter Thiel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:43.440
and got him to invest $20 million dollars&nbsp;
in the company. Now this might sound like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:48.480
lot of money for most startups, but at SpaceX,&nbsp;
it would only cover a few months of expenses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:53.040
so they had to work quickly. There was a glimmer&nbsp;
of hope on the horizon though… NASA was about to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:59.280
award a massive $1.6 billion dollar contract for&nbsp;
12 supply runs to the International Space Station.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:03.760
All the usual big names would be competing for&nbsp;
the contract, but NASA was open to giving a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:08.480
startup like SpaceX a real shot. This was the&nbsp;
chance that Elon needed to save the company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:12.800
but there was no way he could win the contract&nbsp;
without a successful launch under his belt. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:18.160
was no time to build an entirely new Falcon 1 from&nbsp;
scratch, but SpaceX did have a spare rocket in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:23.120
LA factory. So the team packed up the rocket and&nbsp;
rented a military cargo plane to fly the rocket to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:27.840
the launch site. A small group of SpaceX employees&nbsp;
rode in the plane with the rocket, and it’s good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:32.640
that they did. As the plane started to descend,&nbsp;
they noticed that dents were starting to form in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:37.440
the rocket’s body. The pressure levels inside the&nbsp;
rocket were destabilizing and it could implode at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:42.320
any minute. So one of the engineers stepped up to&nbsp;
fix the problem, and in doing so, probably saved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.320 --> 00:18:47.280
the entire company. He climbed inside the rocket&nbsp;
and used a wrench to open a valve that equalized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:51.840
the pressure. The plane landed safely and the&nbsp;
rocket was still in one piece, so after a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:56.800
minor repairs, SpaceX was ready to launch. This&nbsp;
time around, everything worked perfectly. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.800 --> 00:19:01.680
was Elon’s last chance to keep SpaceX alive and&nbsp;
he’d done it. SpaceX had just become the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:06.320
commercial company in history to design, build,&nbsp;
and launch a liquid-fueled rocket to orbit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:14.640
That fourth launch marked a turning point for&nbsp;
SpaceX. Just a few months later, Elon and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:20.320
brother Kimbal were on a family trip to Disneyland&nbsp;
when Elon received a call. It was NASA. SpaceX&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:25.040
had just won the $1.6 billion dollar contract.&nbsp;
This changed everything for SpaceX. Even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.040 --> 00:19:29.760
they wouldn’t receive all the money upfront, it&nbsp;
provided predictable cash flow and allowed them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:35.040
attract investors and employees. SpaceX was now a&nbsp;
real space company and they could start working on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:40.160
bigger projects like the Falcon 9, Dragon capsule,&nbsp;
and Starship. I love the story of SpaceX because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.160 --> 00:19:44.720
it’s all about perseverance. Watching a rocket&nbsp;
that you spent years designing and building go up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.720 --> 00:19:50.240
in flames just a few minutes after launch has to&nbsp;
be crushing. But Elon never let that get him down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:55.440
He always found a way to get one more chance,&nbsp;
because he knew what he was building was worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.440 --> 00:20:00.160
fighting for. If you want to learn more about&nbsp;
SpaceX, just drop your email at johncoogan.com&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.160 --> 00:20:05.840
and please check out this recommended video, the&nbsp;
YouTube algorithm thinks you’ll really like it.

